WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
VOICE OVER: Why do some animals&nbsp;
live so much longer than others?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.000
And what is it that causes the&nbsp;
variation in the ageing process?

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.800
One theory suggests that somatic mutations

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.280
— changes that accrue in our bodies&nbsp;over time,
could be part of the answer.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.680
ALEX CAGAN: So there's you know good reason to&nbsp;
suspect that somatic mutations that are altering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.200
the DNA could be having deleterious effects on&nbsp;
cellular function that could lead to breakdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.200
and kind of consequences that we see in ageing.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.440
VO: But this theory doesn’t necessarily&nbsp;
align with what we see in the animal kingdom.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.680
For example somatic mutations are known to&nbsp;
cause cancers which can in turn reduce lifespan

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.560
— and so it would stand to reason that&nbsp;
larger animals with more cells to mutate —

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.120
might have a higher chance of developing cancer&nbsp;
somewhere in their body and thus have lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.840
lifespans. But in fact the opposite is often true.&nbsp;
With a few notable exceptions, larger animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.360
often have far lower rates of cancer than smaller&nbsp;
ones - a phenomenon known as Peto’s paradox.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.720
ALEX: Everything we know about&nbsp;
somatic mutations and cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.480
we would expect that the more cells&nbsp;
you have the higher your chance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.640
cancer because you just have more cells where you&nbsp;
could be unlucky and accumulate somatic mutations.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.880
VO: What’s more - some of the longest&nbsp;
lived animals in the world are also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.320
largest despite having so many cells which&nbsp;
could be deteriorating through mutations.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.720
If somatic mutations are linked to ageing,&nbsp;
then something else must be going on - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.760
now a team of researchers have delved into&nbsp;
the somatic mutation rates of a range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.840
mammals for the first time - in the hopes of&nbsp;
finally unraveling some of these mysteries.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.280
VO: Somatic mutations can&nbsp;
be caused by lots of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.240
like errors in DNA replication or exposure&nbsp;
to mutagens like ultraviolet radiation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.120
Despite being very common, their&nbsp;
effects are still not fully understood.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.160
ALEX: The most well-known consequence that's&nbsp;
very well established is cancer, but what's less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.400
well understood and still debated is whether&nbsp;
somatic mutations could be playing other roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.880
uh crucially for example what is the role of&nbsp;
somatic mutation in ageing? Because we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.760
as we age we're accumulating somatic mutations&nbsp;
but kind of the key question in the field is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.560
are these a consequence of&nbsp;
ageing or a cause of ageing.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.120
VO: Alex and his colleagues wanted to measure&nbsp;
the rate these somatic mutations were acquired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.280
not just in humans, but across as&nbsp;
wide a variety of mammals as possible.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.560
So they set about collecting tissue samples.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.320
ALEX: so we work with a variety of partners&nbsp;
including the London Zoo so ZSL Institute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.600
and the CSIP which is the Cetacean Stranding&nbsp;
Investigation Program who i'd never heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.920
of before but they're essentially like CSI&nbsp;
for cetaceans or whales and so we work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.640
partners like that and different zoos to collect&nbsp;
tissue samples opportunistically from when animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.640
have died from other causes and so we wanted to&nbsp;
get a tissue type that we could compare across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.040
mammals because we know from human studies that&nbsp;
different cell types in the body have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.000
mutation rates. And so to compare across species&nbsp;
we wanted to pick one cell type and we settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.480
on intestinal crypts which are these small kind&nbsp;
of crypt-like structures that line the intestine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.800
and so basically by sequencing these intestinal&nbsp;
crypts from 16 different mammalian species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.200
and 56 different individuals and knowing the&nbsp;
age of those animals when they died we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.840
count and say okay in this harbour porpoise&nbsp;
that's you know x years old there are this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.920
many mutations while in this mouse you know&nbsp;
that's maybe a few months old we count this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.440
many of mutate number of mutations and then&nbsp;
we can figure out the mutation rate across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.360
these different species and see to what extent&nbsp;
they are similar or to what extent they vary.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.080
VO: Their results allowed them to investigate&nbsp;
whether mutation rates can help explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.720
Peto’s Paradox - the curious observation&nbsp;
that larger animals don’t appear to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.240
more cancers despite having more cells to&nbsp;
mutate. And it turns out that – size is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.640
actually a fairly poor predictor&nbsp;
of the rate of somatic mutation.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.440
For instance cows, giraffes and horses&nbsp;
weigh much more than an average human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.960
but their somatic mutation rates&nbsp;
don’t follow the same pattern.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.240
It’s clear that having more cells doesn’t mean&nbsp;
you have a higher chance of developing cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.960
but the weak correlation between body mass&nbsp;
and somatic mutation rates across species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.920
suggest that lower mutation rates are&nbsp;
not behind this - and that large animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.240
like elephants, must have another&nbsp;
trick for lowering their cancer risk.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.600
However, there was one factor&nbsp;
which seemed to correlate strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:29.200
with somatic mutations, a factor that&nbsp;
is very relevant to ageing: lifespan.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.160
ALEX: By far for us the most striking finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.880
was we found this strong inverse correlation&nbsp;
between lifespan and somatic mutation rate.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.920
VO: For example, the naked mole rat is about&nbsp;
23 thousand times smaller than a giraffe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.640
but they both live for around 20 to 30 years. And&nbsp;
this new research presents an explanation for such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:57.280
similar lifespans in such different creatures -&nbsp;
they have nearly identical somatic mutation rates.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.720
ALEX: And so what that tells us is that somatic&nbsp;
mutation rate is evolutionarily constrained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.880
so there's some reason that evolution&nbsp;
is shaping somatic mutation rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.680
um to be similar at the end of lifespan across&nbsp;
species and this is consistent with the somatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.000
mutational theory of ageing that somatic mutations&nbsp;
play a causative role in the ageing process.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.240
VO: Across the animals tested - somatic mutation&nbsp;
rates proved a reliable predictor of lifespan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.560
and that provides strong evidence&nbsp;
for the theory that somatic mutations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.920
are a cause of ageing, not a result of&nbsp;
it. But the team isn't finished yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.280
they want to expand their study as far and&nbsp;
wide across the tree of life as possible.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.240
ALEX: From evolutionary theory and what we're&nbsp;
seeing in mammals species with longer lifespans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.240
seem to have lower mutation rates so if we can&nbsp;
start to understand how species with even longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.440
lifespans than humans are maintaining their genome&nbsp;
integrity and lowering their mutation rate there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.000
a potential to kind of learn from the solutions&nbsp;
that nature and evolution have already found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.120
uh to potentially find ways to lower the&nbsp;
somatic mutation rate. You know whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.520
that will one day be possible I don't know but&nbsp;
in principle if one could lower the somatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.920
mutation rate that could be a very effective&nbsp;
way to do things like lower cancer risk or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.440
you know slow the ageing process but so&nbsp;
that's you know that's kind of the moonshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:19.840
far future thinking of how this kind of&nbsp;
research could potentially be relevant.

